---
// Full class names on purpose: Tailwind only keeps classes it can see written out.
const labels = [
  {
    name: "Observed",
    tone: "text-ev-observed border-ev-observed",
    body: "The harness recorded it: a tool call, an edit, an exit code.",
  },
  {
    name: "Declared",
    tone: "text-ev-declared border-ev-declared",
    body: "The agent said it. Shown as its own claim, never as proof.",
  },
  {
    name: "Inferred",
    tone: "text-ev-inferred border-ev-inferred",
    body: "Reconstructed from the available records. Part of the model; v0.2 does not produce it yet.",
  },
  {
    name: "Unknown",
    tone: "text-ev-unknown border-ev-unknown",
    body: "There is not enough evidence. WHY says so instead of guessing.",
  },
] as const;
---

<section
  id="evidence"
  class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-28"
  aria-labelledby="evidence-title"
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h2
      id="evidence-title"
      data-reveal
      class="font-display text-4xl leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
    >
      Every explanation says how it is <em class="text-accent">backed</em>
    </h2>
    <p data-reveal class="text-muted mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      WHY labels each piece of evidence, and never presents a guess as fact. In v0.2 the labels you
      will see are Observed, Declared and Unknown.
    </p>
  </div>
  <dl class="mt-12 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {labels.map((label) => (
      <div data-reveal class="border-line bg-surface rounded-3xl border p-7">
        <dt>
          <span class={`rounded-full border px-3 py-1 font-mono text-sm ${label.tone}`}>
            {label.name}
          </span>
        </dt>
        <dd class="text-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">{label.body}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>
  <p data-reveal class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed">
    Unknown beats a confident guess. A tool call that started and never reported a result is shown
    as unknown, never as success. A change that no captured agent edit explains is shown as exactly
    that, because WHY cannot tell a human from a shell command or a lost event.
  </p>
</section>
